[tr][td][b]layer[/b][/td][td][b]test[/b][/td][td][b]result[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]chain[/td][td]tip hash vs oracle at 967,450[/td][td][b]identical[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]UTXO set[/td][td][tt]gettxoutsetinfo muhash[/tt], height pinned on both sides[/td][td][b]identical[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]supply[/td][td][tt]total_amount[/tt][/td][td][b]identical[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]set size[/td][td][tt]txouts[/tt][/td][td][b]identical[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]history[/td][td]same test replayed at height 200,000[/td][td][b]identical[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]txindex[/td][td][tt]getrawtransaction[/tt] by txid alone, block 200,000[/td][td]blockhash matches Core[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]coinstatsindex[/td][td]muhash at an old height, from the index[/td][td]matches Core[/td][/tr]
[/table]

At the tip, height 967,450, both nodes:

[code]
bestblock     00000000000000000002065f1f1fe98d2341da819377fbaa4f940c881dcc0fc7
txouts        165,222,924
total_amount  20085554.25431578
muhash        a464db6b461209f2c14871a9b27013cde9add56b7644419f2f45c42fe806f9b1
[/code]

And replayed at height 200,000, to show this is not a tip-only coincidence:

[code]
txouts        2,318,056
total_amount  9999889.98361183
muhash        169c05b5de13ef28aa2c4e0cb9085502603e9d274babb84872349af7fa3c8ced
[/code]

Both heights were [b]pinned on both sides[/b]. An earlier report in this project claimed a UTXO divergence that turned out to be a torn read of a live, still-applying set; every set comparison here names an explicit height so neither side can be hashing something in motion.

[hr]
[size=12pt][b]The machine, and the configuration[/b][/size]

[table]
[tr][td]CPU[/td][td]AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D, 16 cores / 32 threads[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]RAM[/td][td]123 GB[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]kernel[/td][td]Linux 7.0.0-31-generic[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]datadir[/td][td]NVMe-backed LVM volume, 3.6 TB[/td][/tr]
[/table]

[code]
dbcache=8192          bmc.catchupworkers=8      maxconnections=48
txindex=1             coinstatsindex=1          blockfilterindex=1
addrindex=1           txospenderindex=1         bmc.indexrunblocks=20000
[/code]

[tt]bmc.catchupworkers=8[/tt] is the download concurrency, matched to Core's [tt]MAX_OUTBOUND_FULL_RELAY_CONNECTIONS = 8[/tt], which is not configurable. The log confirms it held: [tt]connected 6/8 peer(s)[/tt], [tt]connected 7/8 peer(s)[/tt]. Peer concurrency is where IBD comparisons usually go wrong, so it is controlled here.

[hr]
[size=12pt][b]Timeline, stated honestly[/b][/size]

[table]
[tr][td]fresh start from genesis[/td][td]2026-09-16 21:10:55 UTC[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]download complete (967,385/967,385)[/td][td]2026-09-17 16:45:16 UTC[/td][/tr]
[tr][td][b]wall clock[/b][/td][td][b]19h 34m[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]node-up time[/td][td]19h 22m[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]downtime[/td][td]11m 33s across 9 restart gaps[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]boots[/td][td]10[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]average throughput[/td][td]10.7 MB/s[/td][/tr]
[/table]

[b]This is not a benchmark-grade wall clock, and it should not be quoted as one.[/b] Run 26 was restarted ten times. Nine of those were deploy-and-debug cycles on the evening of 09-16 while defects found [i]by[/i] the run were being fixed; the tenth, at 08:12 on 09-17, was a deliberate binary swap to pick up two fixes. The binary therefore changed underneath the run. A clean single-binary IBD number needs a run that is left alone from genesis to tip.

For scale only, the last clean Core baseline measured on this box (2026-09-13, unhandicapped, 8 peers, no [tt]coinstatsindex[/tt]) finished 966,808 blocks in [b]19h 14m[/b]. Run 26 is within about 2% of that while building five indexes. Two caveats keep that from being a headline: run 26's restarts, and the fact that the Core baseline's [tt]txindex[/tt]/[tt]blockfilterindex[/tt] settings were not recorded at the time -- only that it carried no [tt]coinstatsindex[/tt]. The honest statement is [i]comparable wall clock with substantially more index work[/i], not a measured win.

[hr]
[size=12pt][b]The result this run existed to prove[/b][/size]

Before this work, a fresh sync ended with four things still to build, by hand or by a supervisor that refused to start until IBD was over: [tt]txindex[/tt] and [tt]txospenderindex[/tt] (each a full walk of the archive), the address history (three passes, ~700 GB of temp), and the block filters. [tt]coinstatsindex[/tt] was invalidated at height 91,842 by the first BIP30 duplicate coinbase and stayed gone.

Run 26 ended with all five at the tip and [b]nothing queued[/b]:

[code]
txindex                    synced=True   967,450
txospenderindex            synced=True   967,450
basic block filter index   synced=True   967,450
addressindex               synced=True   967,450
coinstatsindex             synced=True   967,450
[/code]

The trailing builders' state at the tip is the same state they held mid-sync:

[code]
[trail] txindex: runs reach 959999; the next run starts once 12693 more heights
        are ready | txospender: ... | addr_hist: ...
[/code]

The IBD boundary was a non-event. [tt]index_repair[/tt], the supervisor that used to wait for the sync and then walk the whole archive, never logged a line.

Work done during the sync, by the trailing builders:

[table]
[tr][td][b]index[/b][/td][td][b]runs built[/b][/td][td][b]merges[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]txindex[/td][td]48[/td][td]8[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]txospender[/td][td]48[/td][td]8[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]addr_hist[/td][td]47[/td][td]9[/td][/tr]
[/table]

[hr]
[size=12pt][b]Disk, both nodes, measured[/b][/size]

[table]
[tr][td][b][/b][/td][td][b]bmc run 26[/b][/td][td][b]Core oracle[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td][tt]blk*.dat[/tt] (blocks)[/td][td][b]716.6 GiB[/b][/td][td][b]716.6 GiB[/b][/td][/tr]
[tr][td][tt]rev*.dat[/tt] (undo)[/td][td]246.6 GiB[/td][td]100.0 GiB[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]txindex[/td][td]26.2 GiB[/td][td]68.8 GiB[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]block filter index[/td][td]12.2 GiB[/td][td]12.3 GiB[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]coinstatsindex[/td][td]1.8 GiB[/td][td]0.2 GiB[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]UTXO set on disk[/td][td]12.9 GiB (LSM runs)[/td][td]10.6 GiB (chainstate)[/td][/tr]
[tr][td]address history + tail[/td][td]203.5 GiB[/td][td][i]no equivalent[/i][/td][/tr]
[tr][td]txospender index[/td][td]91.5 GiB[/td][td][i]no equivalent[/i][/td][/tr]
[tr][td][b]total datadir[/b][/td][td][b]1312.3 GiB[/b][/td][td][b]917.6 GiB[/b][/td][/tr]
[/table]

Three things worth reading off that table:

[list]
[li][b]The block data matches to the tenth of a GiB.[/b] Both nodes store 716.6 GiB of [tt]blk*.dat[/tt]. That is an independent check on the archive alongside the hash comparison.[/li]
[li][b]The txindex is 2.6x smaller than Core's[/b] -- 26.2 GiB against 68.8 GiB -- because it is a set of sorted runs with a sparse index, not a LevelDB keyspace. The filter index is a wash and [tt]coinstatsindex[/tt] is larger.[/li]
[li][b]The undo store is 2.5x Core's[/b], 246.6 GiB against 100.0 GiB, and that is a deliberate, load-bearing cost. This node keeps each spent prevout's script and value in its block's undo record, which is what makes a height range self-contained -- and that is precisely what makes the address history buildable in runs during the sync instead of by a whole-chain join afterwards. The 700 GB of temp the old builder needed existed because it had no undo to read.[/li]
[/list]

Net of the two indexes Core has no equivalent for, the datadir is 1017.2 GiB against Core's 917.6 GiB, and the difference is almost entirely that undo store.

